WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.370
You drew the garden on graph paper:

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.930
two variables, three lines.

3
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.250
Our school timetable has

4
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.410
hundreds of choices.

5
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.580
Nobody can draw that.

6
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.270
So today, you’ll tell a computer instead.

7
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.080
Today: tell the computer, with

8
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.500
your first model in Python.

9
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.480
Google’s free toolkit, OR-Tools, has a

10
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.180
solver that searches for you.

11
00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.890
You write the model: the

12
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.970
variables, the limits and the goal.

13
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.850
The solver finds the best plan.

14
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.600
It runs small models right here on

15
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.540
your phone, and the same program

16
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.920
runs on big computers.

17
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.940
First, make a model and its variables.

18
00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.650
Each one is a whole number of

19
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.350
beds, from zero to ten.

20
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.270
Each limit is one line,

21
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:53.450
just like on paper.

22
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.800
Then the goal: make thirty x plus

23
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.260
forty y as big as possible.

24
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.030
Quick check!

25
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.400
What does this line say: model dot add, x

26
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.200
plus y, less than or equal to eight?

27
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.890
x plus y is at most eight: no

28
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.330
more than eight beds in all.

29
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.290
Read less than or equal as at most.

30
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.870
And read greater than or

31
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.610
equal as at least.

32
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.180
Then the solver solves the model,

33
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.540
and you print the answer.

34
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.010
It prints three and five: the

35
00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.050
same corner you drew.

36
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.450
The solver also tells you what it found.

37
00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.530
OPTIMAL means the best plan.

38
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.020
INFEASIBLE means no plan keeps every

39
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.300
rule: you asked for the impossible.

40
00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.690
The solver obeys only the rules you write.

41
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.130
If every class should get a

42
00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.030
bed, write it as a rule.

43
00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.620
If you forget, the solver

44
00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.680
won’t remind you.

45
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.700
In the app, open the timetable

46
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.560
example and press Run.

47
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.510
Then add one rule: class five A

48
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.270
has art in period four.

49
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.780
Run it again, and watch the

50
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.540
timetable rearrange itself.

51
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:16.640
Your turn.

52
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.800
The solver prints INFEASIBLE.

53
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.300
What does that mean?

54
00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.540
No plan keeps all the rules:

55
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.580
one rule has to change.

56
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.960
Remember: variables, limits

57
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.500
and a goal, then solve.

58
00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.110
Less than or equal means at most.

59
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.500
And the solver only knows

60
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.480
what you tell it.

61
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.800
Try it yourself: in the modeller

62
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.000
lab, open the truck example.

63
00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.530
Change the capacity to eleven hundred, and

64
00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.590
guess what changes before you run it.

65
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:52.760
Were you right?
